mentEvent__body-regular"><div class=3D"euiText euiMarkdownFormat css-13tmuh=
d-euiText-s-euiTextColor-default-euiMarkdownFormat-s-default"><p>HI Olamide=
,</p>
<p>Thanks for tracking down the deletion. That confirms the source of the i=
ssue, and knowing that those snapshots are gone also means we probably cann=
ot restore your indices. A snapshot of a mounted searchable snapshot index =
does not contain the data, but rather only a pointer to the original snapsh=
ot. See our <a class=3D"euiLink css-165lmxl-euiLink-primary" href=3D"https:=
//www.elastic.co/docs/deploy-manage/tools/snapshot-and-restore/searchable-s=
napshots" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Searchable snapshot=
s</a> docs here.</p>
<p>So ultimately the <code class=3D"euiCode css-w2v5pz-euiCode" data-code-l=
anguage=3D"text">cloud-snapshot-*</code> snapshots can=E2=80=99t rebuild th=
ose indices now that original snapshots are gone. If you have no other copy=
 of that data from before 22 July, then the data is lost, and deleting the =
six red indices is actually the only way to restore the cluster to health.<=
/p>
<hr class=3D"euiHorizontalRule css-2v5za1-euiHorizontalRule-full-m">
<p>As for why there was a delay in this instance between when you deleted t=
he snapshots and when the cluster went red, essentially Elasticsearch doesn=
=E2=80=99t actually check the searchable snapshot repository unless it has =
to. Specifically, a frozen index keeps no full local copy of its data. The =
snapshot in the repository is the data and the node holds a cache of the pa=
rts of that data that have been recently read.</p>
<p>Elasticsearch reads the repository when it starts a shard or when it nee=
ds to fetch uncached data, but otherwise the frozen index and its node neve=
r actually ask whether the backing snapshot still exists.</p>
<p>So the deletion on 22 July broke those six indices under the hood, but t=
hat failure only shows up in the cluster health once the snapshots need to =
get accessed. In this case, it seems like the trigger was shard allocation.=
 Allocation has to read the snapshot, which subsequently 404ed, resulting i=
n the shards being unable to start.</p>
<p>The documentation linked above describes this in the 'Reliability of Sea=
rchable Snapshots' section under this header: <a class=3D"euiLink css-165lm=
xl-euiLink-primary" href=3D"https://www.elastic.co/docs/deploy-manage/tools=
/snapshot-and-restore/searchable-snapshots#back-up-restore-searchable-snaps=
hots" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">https://www.elastic.co/=
docs/deploy-manage/tools/snapshot-and-restore/searchable-snapshots#back-up-=
restore-searchable-snapshots</a></p>
<blockquote class=3D"euiMarkdownFormat__blockquote">
<p>"if you delete the underlying searchable snapshot Elasticsearch will con=
tinue to operate normally until the first cache miss. This may be much late=
r, for instance when a shard relocates to a different node, or when the nod=
e holding the shard restarts."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ultimately this is normal behavior after deleting backing searchable sna=
pshots, though it does mean those indices now also need to be deleted.</p>
<p>Best,<br>
Matthew</p></div></div></figure></div></li><li class=3D"euiComment css-1wla=
