e_summary.60m-default-2026.06.19-000112</code> index, which was preserved i=
n the searchable snapshot to serve as the backing data for <code class=3D"e=
uiCode css-w2v5pz-euiCode" data-code-language=3D"text">partial-.ds-metrics-=
apm.service_summary.60m-default-2026.06.19-000112</code></p>
<p>As for the creation date of July 17th, that=E2=80=99s most likely the ro=
ll over time. When an index is rolled over, it=E2=80=99s actually 'created'=
 again for the purposes of that creation date tracker. This also tracks wit=
h the snapshot associated with that index starting with the date <code clas=
s=3D"euiCode css-w2v5pz-euiCode" data-code-language=3D"text">2026.07.19-</c=
ode>. While the <em>original</em> index was created in June, the rolled ove=
r frozen index was created in July.</p>
<p>I believe it should be possible to restore this index from one of your a=
utomatic snapshots (the <code class=3D"euiCode css-w2v5pz-euiCode" data-cod=
e-language=3D"text">cloud-snapshot-</code> patterned snapshots), which trac=
k <code class=3D"euiCode css-w2v5pz-euiCode" data-code-language=3D"text">pa=
rtial-.ds-metrics-apm.service_summary.60m-default-2026.06.19-000112</code> =
and the other red indices. However it is possible that even though the snap=
shots backed up the index, they didn=E2=80=99t actually save the backing da=
ta available in the searchable snapshot, and the now-missing searchable sna=
pshot had the only copy of that data. If that=E2=80=99s the case, then the =
data is lost and the only thing that can be done now is to delete those unr=
ecoverable red indices so that the cluster can go back to green.</p>
<p>We should attempt to recover the indices if you want to keep them howeve=
r, and if they are impossible to recover, then the error messages we see wh=
ile attempting to restore them should tell us.</p>
<p>Best,<br>
Matthew</p></div></div></figure></div></li><li class=3D"euiComment css-1wla=
